  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA large ridge of high pressure across the South Central US is \nbuilding today with a warm front that is lifting into the \nNortheast. This front will likely stall in NY down into the Mid \nAtlantic setting us up to be in the \"ring of fire\" around the \nlarge ridge. Today will have some shear between 35 and 40 knots,\nthough thick cloud cover and frequent rain showers keeps CAPE \nvalues low (<300) and elevated so while a few rumbles of thunder\nis possible, severe weather is unlikely today. 850 mb flow into\nthe warm front does pose a heavy rain threat today and tonight.\nTraining storms are possible, especially north around I-90, \nwhere the warm front stalls. PWAT values over 1.5 inches would \nsupport higher rainfall rates.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday (potentially into Thursday), the set \nup is better for stronger, more organized storms. With the front\nstalled around or north of the Thruway, there will be more sun \nto allow for better heating leading to more surface based CAPE. \nTuesday looks to be the warmer day, with ensemble probabilities \nnow near 70% for >1500 J/kg of CAPE along with around 40 knots \nof shear. One of the things working against Tuesday is the lack \nof a trigger for storms during the day but even if storms occur \nearly or after dark, there is potential for large hail and wet \nmicroburst. \n\nWednesday has a stronger shortwave passing through. More \nmoisture aloft will likely mean more clouds so that will limit \nheating and overall CAPE development. Still somewhere between \n800 and 1500 J/kg is looking likely. Shear will be stronger, \nhovering around 50 knots and with the shortwave passing through,\na trigger is present to get storms going even without a whole \nlot of CAPE. One thing that has changed in many of the models \nfrom last night is the thermodynamic profile in the lower \natmosphere. Many of the models have a back door cold \nfront/marine layer sneaking in with warm moist flow lifting over\nthe stable layer leading to high elevated CAPE. The stable \nairmass will lower the wind and microburst threat but keep a \nhail threat for any isolated storms. The forcing in place leads \nme to believe that storms will grow upscale quickly once they \nget going in the afternoon/evening with a greater flash flood \nthreat looking more likely. The warm front will still be stalled\nand could be a focal point for training storms. PWATs near 2 \ninches and a strong (~40 to 50 knot) low level jet forming in \nthe evening on Wednesday feeding persistent, training storms \ncould lead to significant flash flooding where they end up \ntraining. Potential for 4 to 6 inches in less than 12 hours is \npossible where the warm front ends up stalling. With low level \nmoisture remaining in place Thursday with more sun, instability \ndevelops again with high shear once again though the lack of a \ntrigger may keep storms from developing but could have another \nday with a hail and wet microburst threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nFor Friday into the weekend, The large ridge in the central US \nretrogrades west with the trough in the northeast nudging into \nthe mid Atlantic. This will help the flow become more northerly \nwith cooler and drier air advecting in. Depending on how cool \nthe 850 mb temperatures are, there could be some lake effect \nrain showers to deal with but for the most part the weekend is \nlooking mostly dry.",
